        "learnerExperience": "Learners open their cumulative 'Probability Model' page and add: (1) the 2×2 SAMPLE SPACE GRID for two coin tosses, with the four outcomes and their probabilities (¼ each); (2) a RULE BOX with the multiplication law 'Independent Events: P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)' and an example from today's data (P(HH) = ½ × ½ = ¼); and (3) a one-sentence note in their own words: 'Two events are independent when… ' Learners leave a space under the rule box labelled 'Kamau's trips' to be completed in Lesson 8.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'You have been building this model since Lesson 1. Today it gets a rule that lets you combine probabilities across events. Open your model page and let's add two things.' Display a teacher model of the grid and rule box on the board as a reference. Circulate and check that learners write probabilities (fractions), not raw frequencies. Cold-call 1–2 learners to read their note aloud. Close by asking: 'Hold up your model page. In the next lesson Kamau gets his formula.' Collect 5–6 model pages for written feedback (rotate whose work is collected each lesson).",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision: the running model page functions as a personal knowledge artefact that makes conceptual growth visible to both learner and teacher. Placing the sample space grid beside the rule box links the symbolic rule to its evidence: every box in the grid is ¼ because the product of two ½s is ¼.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) The 2×2 grid contains all four outcomes. (2) The multiplication law is written in the rule box using correct notation. (3) The note is in the learner's own words and mentions the first result not changing the second's probability. Teacher reviews 5–6 collected model pages after the lesson and gives written feedback."
