        "learnerExperience": "The teacher draws attention to the DQB. The open question from Lesson 9 reads: 'How can we use the tree to answer questions like \"at least one seat is filled\"?' Each learner writes a NEW card answering it in today's language, in 15 words or fewer (sample: 'Add the end branches that fit the question; multiply along each branch.'). Two learners read their cards aloud and the class votes (thumbs up/sideways/down) on clarity. The selected card is pinned under 'ANSWERED — Lesson 10', together with the card 'A tree diagram forces every possible outcome into the open, and the multiplication and addition rules give each outcome its exact probability.' The teacher then pins the NEW open question for Lesson 11: 'Now that we have experimental probability, sample space, mutually exclusive events, independent events and tree diagrams, how do we bring all of these tools together to help Kamau make a final decision about his matatu route?' Learners write a one-line DQB journal entry: one thing now clear, one thing still uncertain (take-home if time runs out).",
        "teacherMoves": "1. Point to the DQB cards from Lessons 1–9: 'What question from Lesson 9 can we now answer?'\n2. Allow 90 seconds of silent card-writing. Do not suggest wording.\n3. Call on 2 learners to read their cards; thumbs vote on clarity. Pin the winning card and the anchor card.\n4. Pin the Lesson 11 OPEN question. Set the one-line DQB journal entry for the last minute or as take-home; read 2–3 without commenting, noting remaining confusions for the Lesson 11 opening.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative DQB Narrative: pinning an 'ANSWERED' card and a new 'OPEN' card in the same move shows learners that knowledge builds progressively. The personal journal entry externalises metacognitive monitoring.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (i) every learner has written a DQB card that references 'add', 'multiply', 'branches' or 'tree diagram'; (ii) the journal entry identifies at least one specific concept (not just 'I understand probability') as clear; (iii) the teacher's scan of 3–4 journal entries reveals the quality of remaining confusion for Lesson 11 planning."
