    "overview": "This 40-minute lesson is the capstone of the entire Probability I unit and gives the game-playing activity a full period. In the Observe phase (game design, exchange and play), groups of 3–4 receive a 'Game Design Brief' card and design a simple probability-based game for a school fete using materials available in a Kenyan classroom (coins, dice or a deck of cards; or a paper spinner or bag of counters). For example, learners work in groups to design a probability game using coins, dice or cards. The group writes on one A3 sheet (or two taped exercise-book pages): (a) the setup and rules, (b) the complete sample space, (c) the theoretical probability of each player winning, and (d) a one-line fairness verdict (fair or not, and how to fix it). Groups then EXCHANGE games and actually PLAY the game they receive for about 10 rounds, record the wins, compare them with the theoretical probability the designers gave and write a short fairness verdict with a calculation. This makes abstract laws concrete and personally owned: learners are not merely applying rules but defending them in front of peers. In the Explain phase two games (one fair, one flawed) are discussed with calculations, and the link between short-run results and theory (Law of Large Numbers, Lessons 2 and 5) is made explicit; each learner then writes a Final Explanation Sentence answering the driving question. The Driving Question Board is reviewed and every question marked ANSWERED or REVISED, and learners complete the final cumulative model and close with a teacher-led return to the original phenomenon.\n\nPacing for a 40-minute period: Predict 3, Observe 19, Explain 7, Driving Question Board 5, Model Building 6.",
