        "teacherMoves": "1. Distribute the Game Design Brief cards. Say: 'Design a game for a school fete using coins, dice or cards. It must be mathematically fair. You have 8 minutes.' Circulate and ask: 'How do you know your sample space is complete?', 'What does the Addition Law say about these two outcomes?', 'What calculation proves it is fair?'\n2. At 8 minutes: 'Stop. Pass your game to the next group. Play the game you receive for about 10 rounds, record who wins, then compare with the designers' probability. 8 minutes.' [Circulate; check that groups follow the rules as written and record every round.]\n3. At 16 minutes: 'Now write your Fairness Verdict: does the recorded result match the designers' probability, and is the game fair? Give a calculation and sign it. 3 minutes.'\n4. Teacher exemplar for support if a group is stuck: a coin game where A wins on heads and B on tails (P = 1/2 each); or the two-dice game on the Predict card, where the sample space has 36 equally likely outcomes. Do not hand out the exemplar before groups have tried their own design.\n5. Cold-call 1 group to share the most interesting fairness finding: 'You have just done what a statistician does: design, play, audit.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Design-Then-Audit (Game Design + Peer Evaluation): designing a game requires learners to produce probability knowledge, while auditing a peer's game requires them to evaluate it: two different cognitive operations that together consolidate the Addition and Multiplication Laws more deeply than passive practice. Playing the exchanged game makes the experimental-versus-theoretical comparison concrete: a short run of 10 rounds will often differ from theory.",
        "formativeAssessment": "During game design, teacher checks that (a) every group has listed a complete sample space (not just outcomes for one player); (b) probabilities sum to 1 across the sample space; (c) the fairness verdict includes a calculation, not just a claim. During the play round, teacher checks that groups compare the recorded wins with the theoretical probability, and notes that short runs (10 rounds) can differ from theory (link to Lesson 2). During verdict writing, check that the verdict is signed and based on a calculation."
