        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Let us hear from two groups about their audits.' Select one group whose game is fair and one whose game has a fairness flaw. After each presentation, ask the class: 'Thumbs up if you agree this game is fair, thumbs down if you found an error. Explain your vote with a calculation.' [WAIT 15 seconds before calling on volunteers.] Return to the Predict card: draw the 6×6 grid on the board and ask learners to count sum = 7 (6 cells) and sums 2, 3, 11, 12 (1 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 6 cells). Ask: 'Which law lets us add these?' [Addition Law: mutually exclusive.] Ask: 'Why did your recorded wins in 10 rounds not match exactly? What would happen with 100 rounds?' Close: 'Write one sentence answering: How can Kamau use mathematics to make smarter decisions even though he can never predict any single trip with certainty?' [1 minute silent writing; cold-call 2 students and write two strong sentences on the board.]",
